package clwd:app-data@0.1.0;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DECISION 1 — a namespace belongs to the APP, not to the caller.
//
// Every caller of one app reads and writes THE SAME data. Caller A stores a
// note and caller B reads it. That is not a leak; it is the feature. The data
// belongs to the application, the way rows in an application's database do.
//
// It is also the opposite of what most authors will assume, because
// `clwd:identity` sits in the same world and is per-caller. An app that wants
// per-caller data must scope its own keys by the caller's subject — build the
// key as "note/" followed by the caller's subject id, so each caller reads and
// writes only their own.
//
// An app that forgets to do that has built a shared notepad, and will find out
// from its users rather than from a compiler. Anything storing personal data
// must key by subject explicitly.
//
// DECISION 2 — no `list-keys` in 0.1.0.
//
// Not for secrecy: within an app's own namespace, enumeration leaks nothing.
// The objection is that it commits every future backend to supporting
// efficient enumeration forever, in exchange for a capability no consumer has
// asked for. Adding it later is additive; removing it would not be.
//
// DECISION 3 — no compare-and-swap, and no other atomicity primitive.
//
// The right shape for one depends on the backend, and no backend exists yet.
// Guessing now produces an operation that is both wrong and unremovable.
// Applications needing atomicity should not assume they have it here.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A per-application key-value namespace.
interface store {
    /// Why an operation could not be performed.
    ///
    /// Note what is *not* here: "key not found". Absence is an ordinary result,
    /// not an error — `get` returns `ok(none)`.
    variant error {
        /// The key does not satisfy the rules on `get`. Carries a description
        /// for a human; do not parse it.
        invalid-key(string),
        /// The value exceeds what this host accepts. Carries that host's limit
        /// in bytes, so a caller can adapt rather than guess.
        value-too-large(u64),
        /// The backing store could not be reached. Retrying may work; the
        /// operation may or may not have taken effect.
        unavailable,
    }

    /// The value stored at `key`, or `none` if nothing is stored there.
    ///
    /// # Keys
    ///
    /// A key is 1 to 128 bytes of `a`-`z`, `0`-`9`, `.`, `_` or `-`, and may
    /// not begin or end with `.`, `_` or `-`. Anything else is `invalid-key`.
    ///
    /// The rules are deliberately tighter than any one backend needs. Every
    /// plausible one — a filesystem, an object store, a key-value service —
    /// has different limits, and a key space that is legal here but not there
    /// becomes a compatibility problem the first time the backing store
    /// changes. Loosening these later is additive; tightening them would not
    /// be, so they start tight.
    get: func(key: string) -> result<option<list<u8>>, error>;

    /// Store `value` at `key`, replacing anything already there.
    ///
    /// Values are opaque bytes: this interface never interprets them, and a
    /// host must not require them to be valid UTF-8. An empty value is legal
    /// and is distinct from absence — a key set to an empty value is present.
    ///
    /// Every host accepts values of at least 1 MiB. A host may accept more; a
    /// host that refuses reports its own limit in `value-too-large`.
    set: func(key: string, value: list<u8>) -> result<_, error>;

    /// Remove `key`.
    ///
    /// Idempotent: deleting a key that is not present succeeds. A caller that
    /// needs to know whether anything was there should `get` first, accepting
    /// that nothing here makes the pair atomic — see DECISION 3.
    delete: func(key: string) -> result<_, error>;
}
